Homework Reflection 9

1. Write some code that will use a simulation to estimate the standard deviation of the coefficient when there is heteroskedasticity.  
Compare these standard errors to those found via statsmodels OLS or a similar linear regression model.

Code: 
n = 1000
simulations = 1000

coefs = []
ols_se = []
robust_se = []

for i in range(simulations):

    X = np.random.uniform(1, 10, n)
    error = np.random.normal(0, 0.5 * X, n)

    Y = 4 + 7 * X + error

    X = sm.add_constant(X)

    model = sm.OLS(Y, X).fit()

    coefs.append(model.params[1])
    ols_se.append(model.bse[1])

simulation_sd = np.std(coefs)

average_ols_se = np.mean(ols_se)
average_robust_se = np.mean(robust_se)

print(f'Simulation Standard Deviation: {simulation_sd:.3f}')
print(f'Average OLS Standard Error: {average_ols_se:.3f}')

When I ran this code, the results were the simulation standard deviation was 0.039 and the average OLS standard error was 0.037. These numbers may differ slightly when ran again due to not setting a random seed. Comparing these numbers, the difference isn't that large, but the actual variance in the coefficients is larger than the OLS model is producing. This is most likely caused by the heteroskedasticity I included in this data, as the regular OLS models assumes there is homoskedasticity, meaning that there is a constant error variance which is not the case here. I also want to note that even though the difference is small, it still shows heteroskedasticity has an effect on the standard error.

2. Write some code that will use a simulation to estimate the standard deviation of the coefficient when errors are highly correlated / non-independent.
Compare these standard errors to those found via statsmodels OlS or a similar linear regression model.

Show that if the correlation between errors is high enough, then the estimated standard deviation of the coefficient, using bootstrap errors, 
might not match that found by a full simulation of the Data Generating Process.  (This can be fixed if you have a huge amount of data for the bootstrap simulation.)

 Code: 

