2. Construct a dataset in which there are three groups whose values each increase discontinuously (suddenly) by the same amount at a shared event; they change in parallel
over time, but they have different starting values.  Create a model that combines group fixed effects with an event study, as suggested in the online reading.
Explain what you did, how the model works, and how it accounts for both baseline differences and the common event effect.

Code: 

time = np.arange(100)
event_time = 50

groups = ['A', 'B', 'C']

starting_values = {'A' : 12, 'B': 18, 'C' : 24}

data = []

for group in groups:
    for t in time: 
        post = (t >= event_time).astype(int)

        y = (starting_values[group] + 0.5 * t + 7 * post + np.random.normal(0, 1))

        data.append([group, t, post, y])

df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns = ['group', 'time', 'post', 'Y'])

X = pd.get_dummies(df[['group']], drop_first = True, dtype = int)

X['time'] = df['time']
X['post'] = df['post']

X = sm.add_constant(X)

Y = df['Y']

model = sm.OLS(Y, X).fit()

print(model.summary())

For this process, I started by creating my three groups and setting the starting values for each. For the base group (A), I started at 12 and then increased by 6 for groups B (18) and C (24). Once I had my starting values set, for each group I would create a post value and then a Y value for time 1 - 100 with an event time of 50. Each group increases at a rate of 0.5 and there is a common event that increases the groups outcome by 7 after the event. Sharing the same rate increase allows there to be parallel trends between the groups before the event occurs. When it comes to accounting for baseline differences between groups, I created a X dataframe with dummy variables for each group, and dropped the group a dummy variable so it is the reference/baseline category. I then ran a linear model and looked at the coefficients to see how well the model does at successfully identifying the shared increase across all three groups. Looking at my results, the coefficients for groups b and c are both very close to the difference they have in the starting values from group A. The coefficient for post is 6.4 which is also close to the true value of 7. The post coefficient is telling me the model's estimate of the common event shared increase across the groups. This tells me that the model does a good job at identifying the common event effect that was included in the construction of the Y value. The small difference that we see in the post coefficient may be due to the random error included in the construction of the Y variable. 

